  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The bullpen unraveled for eleven runs in two innings after Detroit took the lead, and the A's never answered. They held the game for three innings, then surrendered control in the eighth when two relievers gave up eight straight runs. Jeffrey Springs threw four solid innings. Two earned runs, two strikeouts. But the offense managed three hits and seven strikeouts, leaving them with no foundation to build on when the bottom fell out. This is a team running through scoreless chances and getting punished for it.",
    "what_this_means": "The math of leaving runners on base finally demanded payment. They had a runner in scoring position three times and converted none of them, including a wasted opportunity in the third with a runner on. That inning should have been a statement. Instead it was a missed moment they couldn't recover from. By the time Detroit scored in the fourth, the A's were already behind and never found their way back. The bullpen. Juenger held the line in the seventh with two strikeouts in a clean inning, but Brady Basso gave up two in the second and then Luis Morales collapsed completely in the ninth with eight runs on nobody's control. When a starter gives you a reasonable line, you don't survive the night by scoring one run. They wasted the window.",
    "what_to_watch": "Detroit starts Framber Valdez tonight, a pitcher carrying a 4.53 ERA but one who has been sharper in recent weeks. Jack Perkins gets the ball for Oakland. The A's are running out of nights to prove the offense can carry even moderate pitching into wins.",
